Rich Alderson <news@alderson.users.panix.com> writes:
> I just want to check my feeling about parallels between two product lines.
> Is the following roughly correct?  That is, do the two line up about like this?
> 
> 	36-bit		18-bit
> 	------		------
> 	KA-10		PDP-7,
> 			PDP-9
> 
> 	KI-10		PDP-9/L (?),
> 			PDP-15
> 
> 	KL-10		PDP-15/76 (= XVM)


Don't forget the early stuff.  And although the PDP-15/76 was later than
the base PDP-15, it didn't use any fundamentally different technology,
so I'd group it with the KI10 rather than the KL10.


construction 	36-bit		18-bit		12-bit		16-bit
------------ 	------		------		------		------
discrete        166 (PDP-6)	PDP-1,		PDP-5		none
transistor			PDP-4
"system
modules"
(1960-1964)


discrete       	KA10		PDP-7,		PDP-8,		none
transistor,			PDP-9		PDP-8/S,
small						LINC-8
"flip-chip"
modules
(1964-1970)


integrated 	KI10		PDP-15,		PDP-8/I,	PDP-11/20,
circuit, 			PDP-15/76	PDP-12		PDP-16/M
SSI, small     	 					
"flip-chip"
modules
(1970-1975)

	
integrated 	KL10		none		PDP-8/E		PDP-11/05,
circuit,							PDP-11/40,
SSI, large							PDP-11/45,
modules								PDP-11/70
(1975-1977)


integrated	KS10,		none		PDP-8/A		PDP-11/04,
circuit,	(KC10)						PDP-11/34,
MSI/LSI,							PDP-11/44
large
modules
(1977-1982)

VLSI		3rd-party	none		VT78,		PDP-11/03,
(1975-)		only				DECmate		PDP-11/23,
								PDP-11/24


Date ranges are approximate, and there are exceptions.

The SSI/MSI/LSI/VLSI categorization is based on the predominance of
chips in the processor, but does not exclude other levels of
integration.  For instance, there was some MSI in even the early
IC-based processors, such as the 74181 ALU chip in most early PDP-11
processors, and even in the KA10 (bipolar RAM for fast memory).
Similarly, there was some VLSI in the machines that were predominantly
MSI/LSI, such as the AMD 2901 bit slices in the KS10.

There are many other PDP-8 and PDP-11 models; the chart only shows some
representative examples.

Note that the DEC 12-bit family is the only one for which DEC spanned
the entire range of hardware design from System Modules to VLSI.


