Ian, I just went thru pain with a pair of 6600s in our lab.  One had 
died and the other survived after a physical move.  Simple enough to 
move things into the survivor (the REAL XMI-2 machine, the dead one was 
a converted XMI-1).  Suuuuuuuuure, right.  This "simple" process of 
adding and moving stuff around resulted in a second dead box it seemed. 
  Reduce the XMI, try again.  Four plus hours later, I finally reseated, 
prodded, cajoled and cussed it back to life.  Piddling on the XMI was SO 
sensitive to manipulation, everything needed reseating.  I had to hold 
the XMI modules firmly in the backplane and lift up slightly when 
actuating the lever to lock in the cards.  That seemed to help, but it 
was painful and frustrating.

Most VAX6000-600s did NOT have a BI.  Those that did were usually 
converted XMI-1 boxes and had the monster inverter that sat on the far 
right side of the XMI and emitted a very orange glow from it's LEDs.  If 
your system has the inverter and other mods to make it XMI-2, you should 
have a label on the back of the system or in the XMI cage stating the 
"model number conversion."  If you could look on the back and get the 
model number from the system, that might help figure out if you're 
dealing with a native XMI-2 or converted XMI-1 system.

The other thing I noticed with the converted VAX6600 was the power 
converter(?) for the XMI-2 was distinctly different from that in the 
older, converted box.  The newer system had a converter with a bank of 
LEDs and the older box had a power converter that only had a couple or 
three LEDs, all the same color.

You're also listening for an audible, electronic "thunk" when power is 
connected and you turn it to "enable."

bob



